<spell-notebook "space usage">
	<header level="1">
		Space
		<header>A space is a gap between things:  a line break, a gap in a line, or the room left in a row.</>
	</>
	<space-tab-bar showing="usage"/>
	<section>
		<menu class="ui-docs-section-tabs" appearance="segmented" aria-label="Sections">
			<item link="#using-it">Using it</>
			<item link="#accessibility">Accessibility</>
		</>
	</>

	<!-- USAGE:  how to use it (attributes in practice, events, framework snippets), keyboard, accessibility. -->
	<section label="Using it">
		<markdown>
			`<ui-space>` is an empty box with no look of its own:  a gap between things.

			- Bare, it is a line break:  what follows starts on a new line.
			- `inline` makes it a gap inside the line instead.
			- `width` and `height` size it:  a number is pixels, `xs` ... `xl` the theme's space steps, anything
			  else a CSS length.
			- In a row or a column (`<ui-stack>`, a flex box):
			  - `fluid` takes the room left, and gives it back when there's none
			  - `grow` and `shrink` do each half on their own, bare (1) or by a number (`grow="2"`)
			- In a stack, the stack's own spacing still falls either side of it.

			In plain HTML a `<br>` or a margin does the same.
			The tag is for where there's no `<br>`:  a drawing, which writes `<space/>`.
		</>
	</>

	<section label="Accessibility">
		<markdown>
			A space has no role and no text:  screen readers pass over it, as over a `<br>`.
		</>
	</>
</spell-notebook>
